### 5.3 Parsing and normalization
- Supported: `vless://`, `vmess://` (base64 JSON), `trojan://`, including `security=reality`, XTLS
  Vision flow, and the transports supported by the bundled core (Phase 0): tcp, ws, grpc,
  httpupgrade, xhttp, h2. Other schemes (ss://, hysteria2://, tuic://, wireguard://) are counted as
  unsupported and skipped.
- Tolerate real-world quirks: URL-encoded values, missing base64 padding, uppercase schemes, CRLF and
  trailing whitespace, IPv6 literals in brackets, emoji/RTL text in fragments, alternative parameter
  names (`sni`/`peer`, `allowInsecure`/`insecure`), numbers given as strings in vmess JSON, WS paths
  with `?ed=2048`, gRPC `serviceName`/`mode`, comma-separated `alpn`.
- Malformed or unsupported entries go into a rejection-reason histogram and are skipped. The parser
  must never crash on any input (fuzz it).
- Normalize into one typed model: protocol, host, port, credential (uuid/password), flow, encryption,
  network, security, sni, alpn, fingerprint, reality public key / short id / spider path, host
  header, path, gRPC service/mode. Discard the original remark.
- Canonical ID: SHA-256 over an ordered serialization of ONLY connection-relevant fields
  (lowercased/punycoded host, port, credential, flow, network, security, sni, host header, path,
  service name, pbk, sid, fp, sorted alpn). Remarks never influence it. Use it to deduplicate across
  all sources. `id` = first 16 hex chars.
- Diversity cap: at most 3 configs per (host, port).

### 5.4 Static validation (drop on any failure)
- Host: valid RFC 1123 hostname (<= 253 chars, labels <= 63) or IP literal. Reject loopback,
  private (RFC 1918), link-local (including 169.254.0.0/16), CGNAT (100.64.0.0/10), multicast,
  unspecified, reserved/documentation ranges, IPv4-mapped IPv6 of those, `localhost`, `.local`,
  `.internal`, `.lan`, `.home.arpa`. In the pipeline, also reject hostnames that RESOLVE into those
  ranges (prevents LAN pivoting and probing the CI runner's internal network).
- Port 1..65535.
- Credentials: VLESS/VMess id must be a valid UUID; Trojan password 1..256 printable chars.
  VMess: alterId must be 0 and `scy` must not be `none`/`zero`. VLESS `encryption` must be `none`
  unless Phase 0 shows the bundled core and generator support more.
- Security policy (configurable; defaults shown):
  - Allowed: `tls`, `reality`. Reject VLESS with `security=none`; reject Trojan without TLS/Reality.
  - Reject `allowInsecure=true` (flag `ALLOW_INSECURE_TLS=false`).
  - Reality requires: `pbk` (base64url x25519 public key, 43 chars), non-empty valid `sni`, `fp` from
    the set the bundled core accepts (chrome, firefox, safari, ios, android, edge, 360, qq, random,
    randomized...), optional `sid` (even-length hex, <= 16 chars), optional `spx` (<= 64 chars),
    `flow` empty or `xtls-rprx-vision`.
  - TLS: `sni` defaults to host when host is a domain; `alpn` subset of {h2, http/1.1, h3}; `fp`
    from the allowed set.
- Transport whitelist = exactly what the bundled core and the existing config generator support.
- String hygiene: length caps on every field (path <= 256, sni/host <= 253, service name <= 128);
  reject control characters (U+0000-U+001F, U+007F); reject non-ASCII hosts (convert IDN to
  punycode first).
- Implement these rules in ONE module with shared test vectors in `free-config-pipeline/testdata/`.
  The Android unit tests must load the same files (Gradle test-resources srcDir pointing there, no
  duplicated copies) so pipeline and app behave identically.

### 5.5 Testing on the runner
Goal: a real but coarse pre-filter and a prior for ranking. IMPORTANT: the runner sits in a
datacenter network. A server that works there may be unreachable from the end user's censored
network, and vice versa. Thresholds are therefore generous, results are only a prior, and the
on-device test (Part C) is authoritative.
- Stage A (cheap, async, high concurrency): DNS resolve + TCP connect (timeout 3 s). Drop failures.
- Stage B (real): run the official Xray-core release (pinned `XRAY_VERSION`, SHA-256 verified,
  feature-compatible with the version bundled in the app per Phase 0). Build client configs
  programmatically with a JSON library (never string templates). Batch: one Xray process per batch
  (<= 100 configs), each config on its own loopback SOCKS inbound mapped to its own outbound through
  `inboundTag -> outboundTag` routing. For every config run 3 rounds spaced 5-10 s apart:
  1. GET `https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204` (expect 204 and empty body; fallback
     `https://cp.cloudflare.com/generate_204`); measure time to first byte.
  2. Integrity canary: fetch `canary.txt` from our own Pages site over HTTPS through the proxy and
     compare its SHA-256 with the expected hash stored in the repo (detects tampering, injection,
     captive responses). Full TLS certificate verification must succeed through the tunnel. If the
     canary is not published yet (first run), skip this check and log it.
  3. Optional small throughput sample (<= 500 KB).
  Reject on: wrong status/body, canary mismatch, TLS verification failure, success ratio < 2/3,
  median latency > 3000 ms. Record `latency_ms` (median TTFB), `jitter_ms`, `success_ratio`,
  `throughput_kbps`.
- Hard time budget for the whole run (default 25 min); stop gracefully and publish what finished.
- Persist history across runs on the data branch (`state/history.json`): first_seen, last_seen,
  last_ok, rolling success bits for the last 48 runs, EWMA latency. Expire a server after 6
  consecutive failed runs or 48 h without success.
- Publish ranking: score 0..100 = 100 * (0.45*success_ratio_history + 0.30*latency_score +
  0.15*stability + 0.10*throughput_score), latency_score = clamp((2000 - latency_ms)/(2000 - 80),
  0, 1). Keep the top N (default 300) while enforcing diversity: no single (protocol, network,
  security) class above 50% of the list when alternatives exist. Never rank by geography; do no
  geo-IP lookups.

### 5.6 Manifest, integrity and signing
- Publish `manifest.v1.json` and `manifest.v1.json.sig`. Define `manifest.schema.json`; the pipeline
  validates its own output against it.
- Manifest fields: `schema` (1), `sequence` (monotonic integer), `generatedAt` (UTC ISO-8601),
  `validUntil` (generatedAt + 72 h), `keyId`, `pipeline` {version, xrayVersion}, optional `probe`
