## 6. PART B - ANDROID DATA LAYER (`FreeConfigRepository`)

### 6.1 Endpoints and keys (configuration, not hardcoding)
- One object `FreeVpnEndpoints` holds the ordered mirror base URLs and the public key(s). Values come
  from `BuildConfig` fields fed by `gradle.properties`/environment, with defaults derived from
  `git remote -v` (owner/repo). The build must not break when they are unset; at runtime a missing
  configuration produces a clear error state ("Free VPN source not configured").
- Release builds fail closed when no public key is configured. Debug builds may use a test key from
  `gradle.properties` or an explicit "allow unsigned" debug flag.

### 6.2 Fetching
- Use the project's existing HTTP client. HTTPS only. No certificate pinning (signature
  verification protects integrity).
- Race mirrors: start with the mirror that succeeded most recently; if no response headers arrive
  within 2 s, start the next one in parallel; the first complete response that passes verification
  wins and the rest are cancelled. Persist a per-mirror success/failure scoreboard.
- Conditional requests (ETag / If-None-Match) per mirror. Read at most 1 MB (enforced while
  streaming). Timeouts: connect 5 s, read 10 s, overall 20 s.
- Assume heavily filtered networks where github.com / raw.githubusercontent.com may be slow or
  blocked. That is why several mirrors, the cache and the seed exist.
- Refresh while disconnected whenever possible. While connected, refresh follows whatever path the
  app's own traffic normally takes (Phase 0, item 4).

### 6.3 Verification order (strict, fail closed)
1. Size check.
2. Signature check on the RAW bytes.
3. Strict JSON parse (depth <= 8, <= 400 servers).
4. `schema == 1`; `keyId` known; `sequence` >= cached sequence (reject rollback; equal = no-op);
   `generatedAt` <= now + 1 day. Age: fresh until `validUntil`; stale (usable only as a last
   resort) up to 14 days after `generatedAt`; older manifests are ignored. The bundled seed is the
   exception: always usable as a flagged, low-prior fallback.
5. Per-server validation with the SAME rules as 5.4 (minus runner-only DNS checks). Invalid servers
   are dropped individually; if more than 30% are invalid, reject the whole manifest and log it
   (bug or attack).
6. Persist atomically.

### 6.4 Cache, seed and local statistics
- Store in app-private storage excluded from backup/data extraction (`noBackupFilesDir` + backup
  rules): `manifest.json`, `manifest.sig`, `local_stats.json`. Write to a temp file, then atomic
  rename. On corruption: delete and fall back to the seed.
- Seed: a signed `assets/free_seed.json` (+ `.sig`, <= 100 servers) used when no cache exists (first
  run in a filtered network). Provide a documented manual script `scripts/update-seed`. The Gradle
  build must NOT need network access. If no seed exists, the app still works.
- Local per-server statistics (separate from the manifest): last probe time, EWMA success, EWMA
  latency, EWMA jitter, consecutive failures, quarantine-until, last connected, last failure reason
  (enum, nothing sensitive). A server absent from the latest verified manifest leaves the working set
  (its local stats are kept 7 days, then pruned).

### 6.5 Refresh policy (automatic, no user action)
- Opening the Free VPN screen with a cache older than the soft TTL (3 h): refresh in the background
  without blocking the UI.
- On Connect with no usable cache: refresh inside the connect budget (blocking, with timeout), then
  fall back to the seed.
- Periodic refresh about every 6 h via WorkManager (or the project's scheduler): network-connected
  constraint, backoff on failure, unique work, KEEP policy.
- A refresh never disturbs an active connection and never triggers a server switch by itself.

## 7. PART C - ON-DEVICE TESTING AND RANKING

The pipeline's results are a prior. The device decides.

### 7.1 Candidate selection
- Working set = cached servers minus quarantined ones. Prior = combination of pipeline `stats.score`
  and local history.
- Pick up to 24 candidates per wave: at most 2 per host, at least 2 different (network, security)
  classes when available, always including the last known good server and the 2 best by local
  history.

### 7.2 Stage 1 - cheap reachability pre-check
- System DNS resolve + TCP connect (timeout 2.5 s, concurrency 12). Treat DNS failure, or answers in
  private/loopback ranges, as failure. Do not work around DNS with hardcoded IPs.

### 7.3 Stage 2 - real Xray test (authoritative)
- Reuse the existing core's own delay-measurement path found in Phase 0 (for example an
  outbound-delay API that spins up a temporary instance for one outbound and fetches a URL through
  it). If none exists, create a short-lived Xray instance per candidate.
- Never expose an unauthenticated local listener. Prefer in-process measurement. If a loopback
  inbound is unavoidable: bind 127.0.0.1, random port, random credentials, closed immediately after
  the probe.
- Probe: HTTPS GET `https://www.gstatic.com/generate_204` (fallbacks
  `https://cp.cloudflare.com/generate_204`, then `http://connectivitycheck.gstatic.com/generate_204`),
  expect status 204 and an empty body, no redirects followed, timeout 6 s. Two samples per
  candidate: `coldMs` (includes handshake) and `warmMs` (steady state). Availability requires the
  first sample to succeed. The signed manifest `probe.urls` may override the URLs (HTTPS only;
  otherwise ignore and use the built-in defaults).
- Concurrency: default 4 (2 on low-RAM devices via `ActivityManager.isLowRamDevice()`), on a
  dedicated bounded dispatcher. Every probe instance is torn down in `finally`: no zombie
  instances, no leaked memory, cancellation-safe.
- Early exit: stop once >= 3 candidates passed and 1.5 s elapsed since the first pass (or the
  remaining ones have a much lower prior). Phase budget 12 s; if none passed, run another wave (max
  3 waves) within the overall connect budget.
- DESIGN RULE: probing while a tunnel is up needs sockets that bypass the tunnel. Do not probe other
  candidates while connected unless the existing engine provably protects those sockets. Therefore:
  test BEFORE connecting; fail over by "try and verify through the live tunnel" using the standby
  queue (section 8); run a full re-test only after the tunnel has been stopped.

### 7.4 Ranking
- Score 0..100 = 100 * (0.50*A + 0.30*L + 0.15*T + 0.05*P) - penalties, where
  A = 0.6*(current probe ok ? 1 : 0) + 0.4*ewmaSuccess (0.5 if no history);
  L = clamp((1800 - warmMs)/(1800 - 80), 0, 1);
  T = clamp(1 - jitterMs/400, 0, 1);
  P = pipeline score/100 (0.5 if absent).
  Penalties: -15 per recent consecutive failure (cap -45); -5 if the server exists only in the seed.
- Keep formula and weights in one file (for example `Scoring.kt`), unit-tested and tunable.
- Output: the best server plus an ordered standby queue of up to 5 servers with distinct hosts,
  preferring a different (network, security) class than the best.
- Rank by measured performance only. Never by country, flag or geo-IP.

## 8. PART D - CONNECTION ORCHESTRATOR AND MONITOR

### 8.1 Ownership and threading
- `FreeVpnOrchestrator` is one state machine with all transitions serialized (single-threaded
  dispatcher or Mutex), state exposed as `StateFlow`, idempotent commands (double taps are safe),
  cancellation-safe coroutines, no leaks. It lives in the same process/scope as the VPN service so
  the session survives the UI going away; the UI is a thin observer.
- Connection ownership token `FREE | MANUAL | NONE`. Auto-reconnect/failover applies ONLY when the
  owner is FREE and the user has not asked to disconnect. If the user connects a normal profile
  while Free VPN is active, stop the free session cleanly and do not fight the manual one. If the
  user taps Free VPN Connect while a manual connection is active, show one short confirmation
  ("Switch from the current connection to Free VPN?") and then replace it.
- Persist `userWantsFreeVpn` so a system restart of the service restores the session; a user
  disconnect clears it.

### 8.2 States
Idle -> Preparing -> (Refreshing) -> Testing -> Connecting(server) -> Verifying ->
Connected(server, ping, quality)
Connected -> Degraded -> FailingOver -> Connecting ...
Connected/Degraded -> WaitingForNetwork -> (resume same server) | FailingOver
Any -> Disconnecting -> Idle. Any -> Error(reason, retryable).
Reasons (enum): NO_NETWORK, NO_LIST, NO_WORKING_SERVER, VPN_PERMISSION_DENIED, ENGINE_ERROR.

### 8.3 Connect flow
1. User taps Connect -> request VPN permission through the existing flow (handle denial gracefully).
2. Load a usable list: cache -> blocking refresh within budget -> seed. Nothing usable ->
   Error(NO_LIST).
3. Run Part C. Nothing works after the allowed waves -> Error(NO_WORKING_SERVER) with Retry.
4. Start the existing engine with the best server (Part E). Verify through the live tunnel within
   8 s: 2 of 2 probes OK (different endpoints; DNS + HTTPS through the tunnel). Success ->
   Connected. Failure -> mark the server failed, stop it, try the next standby (max 5 attempts).
5. Overall connect budget: target <= 8 s with a warm cache; hard limit 30 s, then Error with Retry.
   The user can cancel at any time (same single button).
6. Prefer stickiness: if the last known good server (successful within 24 h) passes a quick probe
   (< 2 s) and its score is within 15% of the best, use it.

### 8.4 Health monitoring while connected
- Measure with the running core's own delay measurement (Phase 0) so no extra listeners are needed.
  Interval 20 s with the screen on, 60 s with the screen off; probe timeout 5 s; on failure re-probe
  once after 2 s against a different endpoint to avoid false positives.
- Ping shown to the user = median of the last 5 successful samples.
- Failure rule: 3 consecutive failed checks -> FailingOver. Accelerate to 2 when traffic counters
  show bytes sent increasing while bytes received stay flat for > 15 s.
- Quality (displayed bucket changes only after 2 consecutive samples in the new bucket):
  Excellent (median <= 150 ms and 0 failures in the last 5), Good (<= 300 ms), Fair (<= 600 ms),
  Poor (> 600 ms or >= 2 failures in the last 5), Offline (3 consecutive failures).
- Failover = quarantine the failing server (cool-downs 2 min, 10 min, 1 h, 6 h), switch to the next
  standby, verify as in 8.3 step 4. If the standby queue is exhausted: stop the tunnel, run a full
  Part C test (probing is safe again), connect to the new best.
- Circuit breaker: after 12 consecutive failed attempts across >= 10 distinct servers, go to
  Error(NO_WORKING_SERVER) and stop retrying until the user taps Retry or the network changes.
- Backoff between recovery cycles: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 30, 60 s (+/-20% jitter), reset after a
  successful connection.

### 8.5 Network changes (do not blame the server)
- Observe default-network changes (use the project's existing network callback if present;
  `registerDefaultNetworkCallback` needs API 24+, otherwise use a network-request callback).
- Network lost: state WaitingForNetwork; pause health checks and failover; do not penalize the
  server.
- Network back: wait 1.5 s to settle, re-verify the SAME server first. If it recovers, resume
  silently. If not within 10 s, fail over, and skip the quarantine penalty when the network changed
  in the last 15 s. Reuse the engine's existing underlying-network handling (for example
  `setUnderlyingNetworks`); do not duplicate it.

### 8.6 Avoiding unnecessary switching
- Switch ONLY when (1) health checks fail (8.4) or (2) quality has been Poor continuously for
  >= 180 s. Quality-driven switches are limited to one per 15 min and are accepted only if the new
  server verifies better than the old server's recent median; otherwise stay, or return to the
  previous server if it still verifies.
- Never switch just because another server has a higher score.
- Minimum dwell time after any switch: 10 min (forced failover excepted).

